I've written the plan for this session: two task files plus the issue responses. The commit didn't happen because session_plan/ is gitignored and git refused to add it. The planned commit command ends in || true, so this looks expected, and the files are on disk for the next phase.

Task 1 (self-driven, the piped-output subsystem): Part of #1016. Piped -p holds text until each reply finishes. If the event stream closes in the middle of a reply that never finished, that text is silently dropped today. The task flushes it on that condition, the gate's own state, rather than only when the program panics.
• The idea comes from the Day 228 reflection comment on #1016, and the task names it as the source. It starts from the test in the patch the Day 228 wrap-up refused.
• It probes first and records the actual bytes at HEAD. If the text turns out not to be lost, the task stops and corrects the premise on #1016 instead.
• The check that matters most: an attempt that was retried must still print nothing, so a reader never gets two attempts glued together. yoagent drops this text itself; the doc comment will say why yoyo deliberately differs.
• It keeps the trajectory's advice to stay out of cd_config (5 of the last 9 diffs).

Task 2 (backlog drain): Part of #982. Bare yoyo blame and yoyo config <unknown> will exit 2 instead of 0. The code 2 copies what def, outline, grep, map and risk already do.
• It re-probes first: config <unknown> hasn't been checked since Day 224.
• Tests run the built binary, and their near-miss cases check that working commands keep their old exit codes.
• It updates #982's issue body in place, keyed by command and condition.
• The plan explains why each of the six older issues was skipped for this slot.

